+## Statement
+
+Assume AC. Let $H$ be a discrete torsion-free subgroup of $\mathrm{PSL}_2(\mathbb C)$, with ordinary domain $\Omega_H$, and write $W=(\mathbb H^3\cup\Omega_H)/H$. Let $C\subset W$ be a compact properly embedded locally flat two-sided annulus, with supplied product and boundary quadrant collars. Its two rims lie in $\partial W$ and have supplied smooth parameters, or finite whole-star piecewise smooth parameters in the analytic boundary structure. They represent an infinite-order class in $\pi_1(W)=H$, and are not freely homotopic within $\partial W$.
+
+Suppose a component $U$ of $W\setminus C$ induces a surjection $\pi_1(U)\to\pi_1(W)$. Then $C$ separates $W$ into two sides. For the closure $T$ of the side other than $U$, inclusion $\pi_1(C)\to\pi_1(T)$ is an isomorphism. Its core is parabolic. The ordinary boundary of $T$ consists of exactly two annular puncture neighborhoods, with their finite rim circles included. They give two distinct isolated conformal puncture ends of $\partial W$. Their peripheral elements generate one full maximal parabolic stabilizer of $H$, up to the base-path conjugacies. In particular the generator is primitive.
+
+The paired ends admit an ACTUAL canonical tube inside $T$ after a compact initial part is removed. With the primitive generator normalized to $z\mapsto z+1$, it is the quotient of $\{(x+iy,t):t\ge0,\ y^2+t^2\ge R^2\}$, including its ordinary tails and excluding the ideal point infinity, for sufficiently large $R$. Its parameter is $S^1\times[R,\infty)\times[0,\pi]$, by $(x,r,\theta)\mapsto(x+ir\cos\theta,r\sin\theta)$. The finite frontier is the annulus $r=R$, and the two ordinary faces are exactly $\theta=0,\pi$. The tube is properly embedded and its complement in $T$ is compact. For a finite family of distinct paired maximal-parabolic conjugacy classes these canonical tubes can be chosen pairwise disjoint, retaining all rim/cusp labels.
+
+No third distinct conformal puncture end of $\partial W$ can have a nontrivial peripheral element conjugate to a power of this generator. Consequently no second annulus satisfying these hypotheses can pair either of these two punctures with a third puncture. Geometric finiteness, a three-manifold compact core and a product classification of $T$ are not assumptions or conclusions.
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** The actual Kleinian quotient, locally flat compact annulus with collars, infinite-order core, nonhomotopic ordinary rims and surjective outside component.
+
+[F1] Collared van Kampen gives amalgamated products for separating cuts and the crossing-generator presentation for a nonseparating cut. Amalgam normal form embeds both factors and makes their intersection exactly the edge subgroup. [[thm-seifert-van-kampen]] [[thm-normal-form-for-free-products-with-amalgamation]]
+
+[F2] A map into a cover lifts under the fundamental-group subgroup condition. [[thm-covering-space-lifting-criterion]]
+
+[F3] The actual discrete torsion-free matrix computations classify nontrivial elements as parabolic or loxodromic. A full stabilizer containing a parabolic is a rank-one or rank-two translation group after normalization, without a finite-volume hypothesis. The upper half-space and ordinary compactifications have their explicit Möbius action. [[lem-local-thin-components-of-torsion-free-kleinian-quotients]] [[lem-complete-curvature-minus-one-manifolds-are-kleinian-quotients]]
+
+[F4] Jordan curves bound topological disks with prescribed boundary extensions. Finite smooth torus circles admit affine normalization and have annular strip parameters, as do disjoint essential circles in a parametrized annulus. [[lem-jordan-schoenflies-extension-for-plane-curves]] [[lem-choice-free-smooth-torus-curves-and-annulus-strips]]
+
+[F5] A bounded holomorphic function on a punctured disk has a removable singularity. [[thm-removable-singularity-characterizations]]
+
+[F6] The actual matrix proofs2.1–4.1 of [[thm-geometrically-finite-kleinian-groups-have-separated-standard-cusps-and-finite-dirichlet-limit-vertices]] prove the following operation without using geometric finiteness: a discrete group containing $z\mapsto z+1$ has $|c|\ge1$ for every nonzero lower-left matrix entry; between two unit parabolic coordinate frames the transition has $|c|^2\ge1$. Given strip bounds $|\operatorname{Im}\Lambda|\le M_i$ in both frames, the exact upper-half-space formula then separates the extended regions $\sqrt{y^2+t^2}\ge2(M_i+2)$. Here the strip bounds will be obtained directly from the paired tails, not from that theorem's GF Statement. No Dirichlet-vertex or bounded-parabolic conclusion is invoked.
+
+[A1] Full choice is [[def-axiom-of-choice]].
+
+## Proof
+
+1.1 In the ball model, radial contraction to an interior point contracts $\mathbb H^3\cup\Omega_H$: at every positive time boundary points have moved into the interior. Its free properly discontinuous $H$ action thus gives $\pi_1(W)=H$. The annulus is injective because its cyclic group generator has infinite-order image. If it did not separate, its normal collar would give a crossing-number homomorphism $\pi_1(W)\to\mathbb Z$: assign zero to paths staying in the cut piece and one to a chosen crossing generator. The collared van Kampen relations preserve this number, and the crossing generator maps to one. Every loop in $U$ has number zero, contradicting the assumed surjection. Thus $C$ separates. It has exactly two sides: its connected two-sided collar meets just two complementary components, and every component meets that collar, since $W$ is connected. Let $A=\pi_1(\overline U)$, $B=\pi_1(T)$ and $Z=\pi_1(C)$. Face maps are injective since their composites to $H$ are. Van Kampen and [F1] give $H=A*_ZB$. The first factor surjects to $H$, and both factors inject. Since their intersection is exactly $Z$, every element of $B$ must belong to the image of $Z$. Hence $Z\to B$ is an isomorphism. Write its infinite-order image as $\langle a\rangle\le H$. [A1, F1, given, construct, contradiction]
+
+2.1 Use [F2] to lift the actual inclusion of $T$ to the cyclic cover $W_a=(\mathbb H^3\cup\Omega_H)/\langle a\rangle$. The lift is injective, since its composite to $W$ is the inclusion of $T$; it is the inverse of the covering restriction over its image. Its only interior frontier is ONE lift $C_0$ of $C$, because $\pi_1(C)\to\pi_1(T)$ is an isomorphism. Thus its interior is a full component of $(\mathbb H^3/\langle a\rangle)\setminus C_0$, not a region bounded by additional unnoticed lifts. Enlarge only the ORDINARY boundary to the explicit elementary compactification $V_a=(\mathbb H^3\cup\Omega_{\langle a\rangle})/\langle a\rangle$. The annulus $C_0$ remains compact proper locally flat with the same collars. Its core generates $\pi_1(V_a)=\langle a\rangle$. It separates $V_a$: otherwise the same crossing-number presentation as in step 1.1 would give an epimorphism to $\mathbb Z$ vanishing on the rim generator, which can be pushed into a boundary collar away from $C_0$. This contradicts generation of $\pi_1(V_a)$ by that rim. There are exactly two components, by connectedness of its collar as above. [F1, F2, F3, step 1.1, construct, contradiction]
+
+3.1 Record the elementary BOUNDARY topology without classifying either three-dimensional side. For a loxodromic $a:z\mapsto bz$, $|b|>1$, logarithmic coordinates identify $\partial V_a$ with $\mathbb C/(2\pi i\mathbb Z+\log b\mathbb Z)$, a torus. Its meridian-longitude map to $\pi_1(V_a)$ kills the first generator and sends the second to $a$. Each rim of $C_0$ therefore has homology $(p,\pm1)$. The two disjoint essential rims are parallel and cut this torus into two annuli. Here is a finite category justification for the stated piecewise smooth rims. At each of their finitely many corners the two noncoincident outward tangent rays have a linear functional positive on one and negative on the other. Projection to this signed coordinate is strictly monotone on a smaller pair of arcs, so the curve is a continuous piecewise smooth graph $y=h(x)$ there. Replace it by a smooth graph agreeing near the endpoints, with arbitrarily small value error. This is a surface ambient homeomorphism isotopic to the identity: in a graph box use $y\mapsto y+t\chi(y-h(x))(h_{\rm new}(x)-h(x))$; make the value error smaller than $1/(2\|\chi'\|_\infty)$, so the map is strictly increasing in $y$, identity at the box boundary and has a continuous inverse. Choose the finitely many graph boxes disjoint and off the other rim. This rounds both rims to smooth circles by a TOP surface isotopy, not a newly asserted smooth ambient isotopy of a nonsmooth curve. Its extension to a supplied boundary collar is explicitly $(r,x)\mapsto(r,h_{\eta(r)t}(x))$, identity at the inner collar edge. Now [F4]'s actual smooth torus normalization gives parallel affine circles and their two annuli; undoing the homeomorphisms proves the boundary-region assertion for the original rims. For parabolic $a:z\mapsto z+1$, $\partial V_a=\mathbb C/\mathbb Z=S^1\times\mathbb R$. The same finite graph rounding and the smooth annulus-strip calculation of [F4], in a compact standard subannulus containing both rims, give a middle c