+
+4.1 Every ordinary boundary region on the lifted $T$ side in step 3.1 lies in $\Omega_H/\langle a\rangle$. Indeed suppose a lift $\zeta$ of one of its points lay in $\Lambda_H$. Choose an interior basepoint of $\overline U$ away from $C$, and a lift $o\in\mathbb H^3$. There are elements $h_j\in H$ with $h_jo\to\zeta$, by the definition of the limit set. Since $\zeta$ is ordinary for $\langle a\rangle$, a compact elementary boundary chart at its cyclic-quotient image has a one-sided interior neighborhood wholly in the chosen $T$ component: $C_0$ is compact and misses this point, and the quadrant incidence identifies this component there. Eventually the images of $h_jo$ in $\mathbb H^3/\langle a\rangle$ lie in that component. Their images in $W$ are all the chosen basepoint outside $T$, a contradiction. This proves the claimed inclusion. The resulting ordinary region is actually in the lifted copy of $T$: it has adjacent interior in that copy, and $T$ is closed with boundary $C$. Its projection to $W$ is therefore injective. If $a$ were loxodromic, step 3.1 gives an ordinary annulus joining the two rims on this side, now entirely in $\partial W$. Its parameter gives a free homotopy there, contrary to the hypothesis. Hence $a$ is parabolic. The same contradiction excludes the side with middle annulus $R_0$ in the parabolic case. The ordinary boundary of the lifted $T$ is thus exactly $R_+\cup R_-$, including their finite rims. [F3, step 1.1, step 2.1, step 3.1, given, construct, contradiction]
+
+5.1 Normalize $a:z\mapsto z+1$ and use the two half-planes in step 3.1. Their quotients by $\langle a\rangle$ are punctured disks by $\exp(2\pi iz)$ on the upper half-plane and $\exp(-2\pi iz)$ on the lower one. Projection to $\partial W$ is injective there by step 4.1. They are disjoint and have compact finite boundary circles, so they define two distinct isolated conformal puncture ends. More explicitly, each sufficiently small punctured disk has just that boundary circle as its frontier in $\partial W$; deleting these two disjoint frontier circles isolates its two punctured components. These punctured neighborhoods escape every compact subset of $\partial W$. Indeed $T$ is a closed cut-side closure in $W$, its lift is a section of the covering over $T$ with continuous inverse given by projection, and its two boundary annuli are closed in its ordinary trace. A hypothetical ordinary accumulation of a sequence escaping along one tail would therefore lift continuously to an ordinary point of that same cyclic-quotient tail. This is impossible in its explicit $S^1\times[M,\infty)$ coordinate. This also shows that there are no extra ordinary frontier points besides their finite rim circles. By [F3], any element of the full $H$ stabilizer of infinity is a translation $z\mapsto z+c$. It carries a sufficiently high upper half-plane to an overlapping upper half-plane. Injection of that punctured neighborhood into $\partial W$ forces this translation to lie in $\langle a\rangle$. Thus the full stabilizer is exactly $\langle a\rangle$, excluding both a proper root and an independent second translation. The finite annular regions from step 4.1 retain each original rim as the boundary of its corresponding puncture neighborhood. [F3, step 3.1, step 4.1, construct, algebra]
+
+6.1 Suppose a third distinct conformal puncture had a peripheral element conjugate to $a^r$, $r\ne0$. Choose its punctured-disk neighborhood disjoint from the two sufficiently far tails just constructed, and conjugate a lift so its monodromy is $a^r$. The covering restriction over that punctured disk has trivial kernel on fundamental groups, because $a$ has infinite order. Its universal lift is therefore a univalent holomorphic map $u:\mathbb H\to\Omega_H$ with $u(w+1)=u(w)+r$. Since the punctured neighborhood downstairs is disjoint from BOTH tails, its full lift avoids their translates and in particular avoids $\operatorname{Im}z>M$ and $\operatorname{Im}z<-M$. Hence $|\operatorname{Im}u(w)|\le M$. The function $\exp(2\pi i u(w)/r)$ is invariant under $w\mapsto w+1$ and descends, via $q=\exp(2\pi iw)$, to a holomorphic function $F$ on the punctured unit disk. Both $F$ and $1/F$ are bounded by $\exp(2\pi M/|r|)$. By [F5] both extend holomorphically across zero, and their product remains one, so $F(0)\ne0$. On a small disk its values are close to $F(0)$ and the convergent power series for $\log(1+z)$ supplies a holomorphic logarithm of $F/F(0)$. Thus its winding around a small circle is zero. But the lift of that circle is a horizontal segment $w\to w+1$, on which the logarithm $2\pi i u(w)/r$ changes by $2\pi i$. Its winding is one, a contradiction. There is no third such puncture. Any other annulus satisfying the hypotheses and sharing one of the first punctures has conjugate full primitive parabolic stabilizer by step 5.1, so its other puncture cannot be a third. This proves the puncture and uniqueness assertions without geometric finiteness or an Ahlfors-finiteness import. [F2, F5, step 4.1, step 5.1, given, construct, contradiction]
+
+7.1 We supply the canonical tubes and their simultaneous separation. The two lifted tails imply $\Lambda_H\setminus\{\infty\}\subset\{|\operatorname{Im}z|\le M\}$. For each of finitely many paired classes choose its unit primitive translation frame and its finite strip bound $M_i$, and put $\rho_i=\sqrt{y_i^2+t_i^2}$. Choose $R_i\ge2(M_i+2)$, increasing it beyond the maximum of $\rho_i$ on its compact original annulus lift. For a transition $g=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}a&b\\c&d\end{smallmatrix}\right)$ between different fixed points (including any group translate of the chosen frames), [F6] gives $|c|^2\ge1$. Its pole $-d/c$ and image $a/c$ belong to the corresponding limit sets. The exact identity $|z'-a/c|^2+(t')^2=1/(|c|^4(|z+d/c|^2+t^2))$ therefore gives $$\rho_i(g(z,t))\le M_i+\frac{1}{|c|^2(\rho_j(z,t)-M_j)}\le M_i+\frac{1}{R_j-M_j}<R_i$$ whenever $\rho_j\ge R_j$. This proves disjointness of the CLOSED regions in all distinct fixed-point frames, even when the transition is not a member of either coordinate group: its conjugate of the source unit translation is in the target group, with lower-left entry $-c^2$, which is exactly [F6]'s proof of the bound. In one fixed-point frame the full stabilizer is precisely the real unit translations by step 5.1, and preserves its region. Thus each region is precisely invariant and the finite quotients are pairwise disjoint. The displayed polar map and its inverse $r=\sqrt{y^2+t^2}$, $\theta=\arg(y+it)$ give the claimed smooth product with ordinary faces, exact circle generator and finite annular frontier. The region is connected and, for the chosen large radius, misses the original $C_0$ and belongs to its tail side. It is therefore inside the lifted copy of $T$ from step 2.1. That copy projects homeomorphically onto the closed original $T$; the region $r\ge R_i$ is closed there, proving proper embedding in $W$. The remaining lifted portion $T\cap\{r\le R_i\}$ lies in a compact cyclic-model cylinder: $x\bmod1$, bounded $y,t$, with only its finite ordinary boundary patches, all in $\Omega_H$ by step 4.1. It is closed in this cylinder, so compact. This proves the exact canonical end realization and its finite-family separation, without classifying a merely abstract cyclic three-manifold or assuming GF for $H$. [F3, F6, step 2.1, step 3.1, step 4.1, step 5.1, construct, algebra] ∎
